> That is not a silly question at all. As long as the families are either both continuous or both discrete, comparisons are possible. If one is continuous and one is discrete, I believe things are more complicated, since the continuous likelhood does not capture probabilities itself, while the discrete likelihood does. Maybe you could also ask this question on http://discourse.mc-stan.org/?

